With a few modifications, it would be a neat addition.

1. First of all, being next to a given monster can't immediately send
you into an indefinite sneezing fit, or else your first encounter with
that type of monster would always be fatal. You'd need some warning
signs.

When the monster is 3 squares away: "You feel your nose begin to
tickle!"
2 squares away: "You feel a need to sneeze!"
1 square away: "Your eyes water, and you begin to sneeze
uncontrollably!"
Then you sneeze up to 10 times. After that, you manage to get some sort
of control over your sneezing fit, enough that you can make one more
move (e.g. to teleport away) before you go off into another sneezing
fit. Repeat indefinitely.

2. Obviously you can't make eating a certain (unknown) monster an
instadeath, or else a new character would be scared to eat anything at
all (besides what they've personally killed, I guess.) But in real
life, you have to be exposed to an allergen at least once (to sensitize
your immune system) before subsequent encounters actually cause bad
effects.
So when your character is created, the monster you'll be allergic to is
selected. The first time you eat that monster, you will be given a
warning message: "You suddenly feel sensitive to X" (where X is the
monster in question.) *Now* if you try to eat a corpse of X, you will
die instantly. (If you have already encountered X and had a sneezing
fit, this acts as the sensitization step and you will die as soon as
you eat the monster.)

3. You can't be allergic to non-organic monsters (e.g. vortices,
elementals, golems), to humanoids of the five player races, to undead
or demons, or to your own pet. (Duh.)

4. Being allergic needs to have at least one good side effect. If the
monster in question is anywhere on the level, every twenty moves or so
you will get the message: "Your nose twitches". This warns you that the
monster you are allergic to is somewhere around.

